{"authored" [{"question" "q-102693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3+8t-3{t}^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "e39c26b45208628dfd3fc441fcf876c2eee59912cc354b70df3aa44388f5ae56" "basis" 1156 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3+8t-3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3t+4{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=-1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =3(0)+4(0)^{2}-(0)^{3}+C \\\\ -1 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3t+4{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}-1.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =3(2)+4(2)^{2}-(2)^{3}-1 \\\\  & =6+16-8-1 \\\\  & =13\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $13\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "e39c26b45208628dfd3fc441fcf876c2eee59912cc354b70df3aa44388f5ae56" "basis" 1156 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/edb-import/verifica