5ae56" "basis" 1156 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "24\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "e39c26b45208628dfd3fc441fcf876c2eee59912cc354b70df3aa44388f5ae56" "basis" 1156 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "e39c26b45208628dfd3fc441fcf876c2eee59912cc354b70df3aa44388f5ae56" "basis" 1156 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) = 4t^2 - 3t^3,$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the velocity (in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s$) of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/example-562.json" "file_sha256" "c385314ab21658200d7db36483d12f774e44c220e54fca2441604e1596a3f9a0" "basis" 1156 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{4}{3}(0)^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = \\dfrac{4}{3}t^3 -\\dfrac{3}{4}t^4 + 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/example-562.json" "file_sha256" "c385314ab21658200d7db36483d12f774e44c220e54fca2441604e1596a3f9a0" "basis" 1156 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) =2e^{-t}+6t^2$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-4 \\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the speed of the particle at the moment  $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/example-563.json" "file_sha256" "2095cb79fd50f936391ec7de85009fbd55b69d5abdbaa8502212b214f4c61521" "basis" 1156 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{-t}+6{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(-{e}^{-t})+\\frac{6}{3}{t}^{3}+C \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-t}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0) = -4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-2{e}^{-0}+2\\cdot {0}^{3}+C \\\\ -4 & =-2+0+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = -2  e^{-t}+ 2t^3 -2.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =-2{e}^{-1}+2(1)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}+2-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{e}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\dfrac 2 e\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/example-563.json" "file_sha256" "2095cb79fd50f936391ec7de85009fbd55b69d5abdbaa8502212b214f4c61521" "basis" 1156 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=12{t}^{5}+5{t}^{4},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=6\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/state.json" "file_sha256" "82cbe2b930ce2a12f8b4d21d24d9b0696867982565bf48602b5d9de689cfc5ca" "basis" 1212 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (12{t}^{5}+5{t}^{4})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{6}+{t}^{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=6.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2({1}^{6})+{1}^{5}+C \\\\ 6 & =3+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{6}+{t}^{5}+3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/state.json" "file_sha256" "82cbe2b930ce2a12f8b4d21d24d9b0696867982565bf48602b5d9de689cfc5ca" "basis" 1212 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e7787218132b17bffca1231384c8545f0b02c15b247e94cc5489cdf213ed24c" "basis" 1212 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346883" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/state.json" "file_sha256" "82cbe2b930ce2a12f8b4d21d24d9b0696867982565bf48602b5d9de689cfc5ca" "basis" 1212 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=5{e}^{t},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/state.json" "file_sha256" "82cbe2b930ce2a12f8b4d21d24d9b0696867982565bf48602b5d9de689cfc5ca" "basis" 1212 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 5{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =5{e}^{0}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =5+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=5{e}^{t}-\\frac{9}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =5{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =5(2)-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/state.json" "file_sha256" "82cbe2b930ce2a12f8b4d21d24d9b0696867982565bf48602b5d9de689cfc5ca" "basis" 1212 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e7787218132b17bffca1231384c8545f0b02c15b247e94cc5489cdf213ed24c" "basis" 1212 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351058" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/state.json" "file_sha256" "82cbe2b930ce2a12f8b4d21d24d9b0696867982565bf48602b5d9de689cfc5ca" "basis" 1212 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351058" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/state.json" "file_sha256" "82cbe2b930ce2a12f8b4d21d24d9b0696867982565bf48602b5d9de689cfc5ca" "basis" 1212 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031307/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-7194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+t$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-10\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-7194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{t}^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=-10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{1}{10}(0)^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ -10 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{10}{t}^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-10.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\\frac{1}{10}(2)^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}(2)^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}-10 \\\\  & =3.2-12+2-10 \\\\  & =-16.8\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $16.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-7194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e452a65bf417c688343b895eee85ac72710dc818771994906dcc8abb1024b16"} {"question" "q-7194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-7194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10.8\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "-16.8\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "10.8\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "16.8\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "7.2\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-7194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16.8\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=6{t}^{2}-2t-10$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6{t}^{2}-2t-10)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-10t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=-4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}-10(1)+C \\\\ -4 & =2-1-10+C \\\\ C & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-10t+5.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=3\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =2(3)^{3}-(3)^{2}-10(3)+5 \\\\  & =54-9-30+5 \\\\  & =20\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $20\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e452a65bf417c688343b895eee85ac72710dc818771994906dcc8abb1024b16"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "15\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "16\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "20\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "24\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-346883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=12{t}^{5}+5{t}^{4},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=6\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-346883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (12{t}^{5}+5{t}^{4})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{6}+{t}^{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=6.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2({1}^{6})+{1}^{5}+C \\\\ 6 & =3+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{6}+{t}^{5}+3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-346883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e452a65bf417c688343b895eee85ac72710dc818771994906dcc8abb1024b16"} {"question" "q-346883" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-346883" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2t^6+t^5+3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=2t+{t}^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the velocity of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{8}{3}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3} & =(1)^{2}+\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3}+C \\\\ \\frac{8}{3} & =1+\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{3}+{t}^{2}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\\frac{4}{3}+(2)^{2}+\\frac{(2)^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+4+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e452a65bf417c688343b895eee85ac72710dc818771994906dcc8abb1024b16"} {"question" "q-102695" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102695" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "6\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "8\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "\\frac{23}{3}\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102695" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3+8t-3{t}^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3+8t-3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3t+4{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=-1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =3(0)+4(0)^{2}-(0)^{3}+C \\\\ -1 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3t+4{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}-1.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =3(2)+4(2)^{2}-(2)^{3}-1 \\\\  & =6+16-8-1 \\\\  & =13\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $13\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e452a65bf417c688343b895eee85ac72710dc818771994906dcc8abb1024b16"} {"question" "q-102693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "11\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "13\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "15\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "17\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "9\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "13\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-81707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=-6{e}^{-2t}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity (in $\\text{m/s}$)  of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-81707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-6{e}^{-2t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2t})+C \\\\  & =3{e}^{-2t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 2 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3{e}^{-2t}-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-81707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e452a65bf417c688343b895eee85ac72710dc818771994906dcc8abb1024b16"} {"question" "q-81707" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-81707" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}+4"] ["math" "v(t)=-2{e}^{-t}+2"] ["math" "v(t)=12{e}^{-t}-10"] ["math" "v(t)=3{e}^{-2t}+2"] ["math" "v(t)=3{e}^{-2t}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-81707" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(t)=3{e}^{-2t}-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-351059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+1}},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{ft/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-351059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =6\\sqrt{0+1}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =6+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=6\\sqrt{t+1}-\\frac{11}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=3\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =6\\sqrt{3+1}-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =6(2)-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{2}-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{13}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-351059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e452a65bf417c688343b895eee85ac72710dc818771994906dcc8abb1024b16"} {"question" "q-351059" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-351059" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{13}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has a velocity $v=1$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity (in $\\text{m/s}$)  of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{1}{0+1}+C \\\\ 1 & =-1+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-\\frac{1}{t+1}+2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e452a65bf417c688343b895eee85ac72710dc818771994906dcc8abb1024b16"} {"question" "q-102745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(t)=-\\frac{1}{t+1}+1"] ["math" "v(t)=-\\frac{1}{t+1}+2"] ["math" "v(t)=-\\frac{1}{t+1}-2"] ["math" "v(t)=\\frac{1}{t+1}"] ["math" "v(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-102745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(t)=-\\frac{1}{t+1}+2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-351058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=5{e}^{t},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-351058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 5{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =5{e}^{0}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =5+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=5{e}^{t}-\\frac{9}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =5{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =5(2)-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-351058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e452a65bf417c688343b895eee85ac72710dc818771994906dcc8abb1024b16"} {"question" "q-351058" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-351058" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-346897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-346897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t+1)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(t+1)^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\sqrt{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & =2\\sqrt{1+1}+C \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =2\\sqrt{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\sqrt{2}-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ C & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2\\sqrt{t+1}-\\sqrt{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-346897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8e452a65bf417c688343b895eee85ac72710dc818771994906dcc8abb1024b16"} {"question" "q-346897" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "q-346897" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{t+1}-\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/state.json" "file_sha256" "fab1a751b5010eee1ba011da0dc5afbc43fd0b766495c8e7049e9d5f0b5f03dd"} {"question" "e-562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) = 4t^2 - 3t^3,$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the velocity (in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s$) of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/example-562.json" "file_sha256" "c385314ab21658200d7db36483d12f774e44c220e54fca2441604e1596a3f9a0"} {"question" "e-562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{4}{3}(0)^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = \\dfrac{4}{3}t^3 -\\dfrac{3}{4}t^4 + 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/example-562.json" "file_sha256" "c385314ab21658200d7db36483d12f774e44c220e54fca2441604e1596a3f9a0"} {"question" "e-563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) =2e^{-t}+6t^2$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-4 \\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the speed of the particle at the moment  $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/example-563.json" "file_sha256" "2095cb79fd50f936391ec7de85009fbd55b69d5abdbaa8502212b214f4c61521"} {"question" "e-563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{-t}+6{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(-{e}^{-t})+\\frac{6}{3}{t}^{3}+C \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-t}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0) = -4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-2{e}^{-0}+2\\cdot {0}^{3}+C \\\\ -4 & =-2+0+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = -2  e^{-t}+ 2t^3 -2.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =-2{e}^{-1}+2(1)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}+2-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{e}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\dfrac 2 e\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038230/example-563.json" "file_sha256" "2095cb79fd50f936391ec7de85009fbd55b69d5abdbaa8502212b214f4c61521"}] "usage" {"q-81474" [] "q-346883" [] "q-102693" [] "e-563" [] "q-7194" [] "q-351058" [] "e-562" []} "basis" 1298 "retractions" [[17592186083835 :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $m/s^{2}$, given by the function $a(t) = \\frac{1}{2}t^{4} - 3t^{3} + t$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v =-10m/s$ when $t = 0s$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t = 2s$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186082377 17592186082378 17592186082379 17592186082380 17592186082381 17592186289897 17592186289898 17592186289899 17592186289900 17592186289901 17592186289928 17592186289929 17592186289930 17592186289931 17592186289932 17592186293097 17592186293098 17592186293104] "immutable_fields" [[17592186082376 :answer-field/correct] [17592186082376 :answer-field/id] [17592186082376 :answer-field/key] [17592186082376 :answer-field/type] [17592186289896 :answer-field/correct] [17592186289896 :answer-field/id] [17592186289896 :answer-field/key] [17592186289896 :answer-field/type] [17592186289927 :answer-field/correct] [17592186289927 :answer-field/id] [17592186289927 :answer-field/key] [17592186289927 :answer-field/type] [17592186293096 :answer-field/correct] [17592186293096 :answer-field/id] [17592186293096 :answer-field/key] [17592186293096 :answer-field/type] [17592186293103 :answer-field/correct] [17592186293103 :answer-field/id] [17592186293103 :answer-field/key] [17592186293103 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
